P0184 High

P0185 NEON parsing performs a misaligned vector write

P0186 [src/impls/neon/stage1.rs:207](/work/crate/src/impls/neon/stage1.rs:207), write at line 226.

P0187 `flatten_bits` selects an aligned vector write when the element offset is divisible by four. That does not establish alignment of the underlying `Vec<u32>` allocation, which only guarantees four-byte alignment.

P0188 Executed evidence: Miri stopped while parsing the ordinary object `{"name":"caf\u00e9","items":[1,null,true,{"x":"line\nbreak"}]}` through `to_owned_value`, reporting “alignment 4, but alignment 16 is required” at line 226.

P0189 Ordinary aarch64 callers can therefore encounter undefined behavior through a safe parsing API. Use an unaligned write or establish alignment from the actual destination pointer. Retain the ordinary-input Miri regression.

P0190 Exhausting the public deserializer panics instead of returning an error

P0191 [src/serde.rs:255](/work/crate/src/serde.rs:255), [src/lib.rs:876](/work/crate/src/lib.rs:876).

P0192 After successfully deserializing `u64` from a `Deserializer` containing `1`, a second safe `u64::deserialize(&mut de)` call should return an exhaustion error. It instead panics with an out-of-bounds index in debug mode.

P0193 Executed evidence: `exhausted_deserializer_returns_error` failed with “the len is 1 but the index is 1.” Source inspection shows numeric deserialization calls unchecked `next_()`; release behavior was not executed.

P0194 Use checked tape access in safe Serde entry points and return `Eof` when exhausted.

P0195 Medium

P0196 Long decimals produce different values depending on their input offset

P0197 [src/numberparse/correct.rs:168](/work/crate/src/numberparse/correct.rs:168).

P0198 With default features, `1.2345678901234567890123456789` parses correctly at offset zero. After 64 spaces, it becomes approximately `5.097733592125637e-10`, instead of `1.2345678901234567`.

P0199 Executed evidence: `float_value_is_independent_of_byte_offset` compared five decimal forms at 140 offsets against Rust’s `f64` parser and found substantial errors.

P0200 The code saves the starting digit’s byte value where it needs a starting position, incorrectly bypassing the slow conversion path. Ordinary documents can silently acquire wrong numeric values. Save the position and add offset-invariance regressions.

